Interrupting a piped -p run could drop text the model had already finished. I confirmed that with a test, fixed it, and committed it as 6b51086d (Part of #1016).

How the text was lost. On a pipe, yoyo holds back each assistant message until it ends, then releases the whole message into a queue at once. The main loop takes only one event from that queue each time around. Its tokio::select! (src/prompt.rs) checks its branches in random order, because it has no biased;. So when a signal is waiting, it can be handled while finished text is still in the queue. In the test, the loop had taken one event and three were still queued, and the interrupt handler recovered "". On Ctrl+C, SIGTERM, SIGHUP or SIGQUIT, that finished text was silently thrown away.

Fix. The new RetrySafeGate::take_unprinted_text() returns everything the gate hasn't printed yet: queued text first, then text still being held, in the order it arrived, each piece once. It goes by what is left unprinted, not by which signal fired. The interrupt path (finish_interrupted_turn) and the panic handler now both call it, so there's one copy of the flush logic instead of two. With the hold off (a terminal, the REPL, --print), nothing ever enters the queue, so those paths behave exactly as before.

Tests (each compares the full output exactly):
• The bug case recovers RELEASED once, and a second flush returns "".
• A finished message followed by one in progress comes out as FIRST_SECOND, in that order.
• Text the loop already printed is not printed again.
• An attempt that was retried is never released; only the live one (LIVE) comes out.
• Nothing left gives "", with the hold on and off.

As a check, I disabled the queue drain, with the required NEUTERED marker, in a single break → test → restore command. The two new queue tests failed, and so did the existing panic-handler queue test, since that path now uses the same function. After the restore, build, all tests, clippy and fmt pass, and no marker is left in the code.

Docs and tracking: ARCHITECTURE.md had two lines calling this gap unchecked. I marked both as superseded and added a Day-238 entry. I also commented on #1016 under the 2026-10-15T01:04Z comment that raised it. The last unchecked item on #1016 stays open: the exit code when the stream closes in the middle of an attempt hasn't been measured end to end.
